<!-- cell: title-page id="16be5309-3fc9-44ed-b51a-504442aad317" title="Notes in the Pockets" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="d89f4d65-e8ad-41a9-8087-3de86fa49ede" -->

The Hollis Street laundromat never closed. Its sign said so in red neon, and Mara believed it, because at three in the morning, after a twelve-hour shift on the cardiac ward, it was the only lit window between the hospital and her flat.

She came on Tuesdays. She liked the hum of the machines and the warm, soapy air, and that nobody there expected her to be kind or quick or right.

The owner sat behind the counter doing crosswords in pen. His name was Felix; it was printed on a badge he never seemed to remember he was wearing. By the window stood an empty green armchair that he wiped down every night and that no customer ever sat in.

One Tuesday her basket came back from the dryer with a man's flannel shirt folded on top. In its breast pocket was a slip of paper: Sorry. Wrong basket. Yours is the one with the cats on the pyjamas. Excellent cats. — F.

She laughed out loud, alone, at half past three. The next week she left a note in the pocket of her scrubs: The cats send their regards. They want to know what eleven across was.

It went on like that through the winter. Notes in pockets, notes in sleeves, once a note tucked into the toe of a sock. He told her about the crossword. She told him about the patient who had asked her to marry him and then forgotten her name. Neither of them said anything in person beyond good evening.

In March the laundromat went dark. A sign on the door said CLOSED FOR RENOVATION. Mara stood under the dead neon with her basket on her hip and realised how much of her week had been built around a slip of paper.

For three Tuesdays she washed her clothes in the hospital basement, where the machines were silent and smelled of bleach.

On the fourth Tuesday the neon was back. Taped beneath it was a note in a hand she knew: Reopening tonight. New dryers. Old crosswords. Please come in.

Inside, everything gleamed. The green armchair had been moved from the window to the counter, beside a second chair.

"It was my wife's," Felix said, before she could ask. "She used to sit there and tell me the answers I'd got wrong. I couldn't move it for six years." He looked at the chairs, then at her. "Eleven across," he said. "Eight letters. Being alone."

"Solitude," said Mara, and sat down.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="c0114066-3c8c-427a-8b47-e15da439f1e4" title="Mara" words="6-42, 163-171, 411-416" -->

A night-shift nurse on the cardiac ward who does her laundry at three in the morning, when the laundromat is the only lit window on her way home.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="1fb7a3f5-9abe-4c46-8c28-3d1d71b9771e" title="Felix" words="72-81, 82-99, 365-374" -->

The laundromat's owner, a widower who does crosswords in pen and keeps his late wife's armchair empty by the window.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="a1af5962-f30a-43d6-bc90-82ed1743e792" title="The Hollis Street laundromat" words="0-5, 47-71, 346-348" -->

The all-night laundromat whose warm, humming room is the one place Mara is not expected to be anything.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="ec987775-1fec-4299-b1bc-eed0656b0471" title="Loneliness" words="100-120, 271-297, 403-410" -->

Both of them are alone, and neither says so: it shows in the empty armchair, in Mara's week built around a slip of paper, and in the crossword clue 'being alone'.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="e3b77bba-844e-4c78-a18c-bf782ea6dd45" title="Love" words="198-205, 326-345, 349-364" -->

The love that grows between Mara and Felix through notes left in pockets, without a word said in person until the end.

<!-- cell: plot id="e5907099-e66a-425b-a132-5b0fd81de3b2" title="Notes in the pockets" words="121-162, 172-197, 198-205, 256-297, 318-345, 403-410, 411-416" -->

A mixed-up basket starts an exchange of notes; when the laundromat closes Mara fears it is over, until a note on the door brings her back for the reopening.

<!-- cell: plot id="222b577b-09da-41aa-a328-de2c750c055e" title="Felix's grief" words="72-120, 349-364, 365-402, 411-416" -->

Felix has kept his late wife's chair empty for six years; at the reopening he moves it to the counter beside a second chair, and Mara sits down.
